// A KNOWN, DELIBERATELY UNCLOSED divergence, not a checkpoint that both
// sides must agree on. down(n) holds 20 locals plus its callee and argument
// slots (22 value-stack slots per frame). At n=900 that is 900 * 22 =
// 19800 value-stack slots, past src/vm.h's STACK_MAX (16384), while the
// call chain itself (about 902 frames) stays well under FRAMES_MAX (1024).
//
// Native rejects this with its own "Stack overflow." (the STACK_MAX guard,
// VM::push) and exit 70, the same controlled path FRAMES_MAX uses. The CLR
// backend mirrors FRAMES_MAX (runtime/clr/src/LoxClosure.cs) but has no
// counterpart for STACK_MAX -- it counts calls, not value-stack slots -- so
// this same program runs to completion on CLR and prints 210, exit 0.
//
// tools/check_clr_probes.sh's known_divergence_probes checks this file
// directly, asserting the divergence stays exactly this shape (native
// fails via STACK_MAX, CLR succeeds) so a change to either side's guard is
// caught, not silently absorbed. The managed backends having no ceiling to
// match native's own is tracked outside this file.
//
// Lives one level below clr-only/, not directly in it: tools/diff_runtimes.py's
// CI step walks both test/translation-probes/ and test/translation-probes/clr-only/
// non-recursively and requires every program's stdout to match (or, for an
// empty-stdout failure on both sides, its exit status to agree on success vs.
// failure). This probe's two sides disagree on both counts by design, so a
// generic byte-for-byte walk must never reach it; check_clr_probes.sh names
// it directly instead, the same reason 31_deep_recursion.lox and
// 35_folded_match_deficit_two_plus.lox sit in clr-only/ rather than
// alongside the rest of the corpus.
fun down(n) {
    var a = 1; var b = 2; var c = 3; var d = 4; var e = 5;
    var g = 6; var h = 7; var i = 8; var j = 9; var k = 10;
    var l = 11; var m = 12; var o = 13; var p = 14; var q = 15;
    var r = 16; var s = 17; var t = 18; var u = 19; var v = 20;
    if (n == 0) return a+b+c+d+e+g+h+i+j+k+l+m+o+p+q+r+s+t+u+v;
    return down(n - 1);
}
print down(900);
